# Trivy gate baseline — the container-image scan's list of pre-existing findings.
#
# ── What this file is NOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It is not a waiver, and it does not hide anything. scripts/scan-image.sh runs
# Trivy twice and applies this file to ONE of the two passes:
#
#   report pass  --ignorefile /dev/null   every severity, every CVE, including
#                                         everything listed here → SARIF →
#                                         GitHub code scanning. Nothing is
#                                         suppressed on the reporting surface.
#
#   gate pass    --ignorefile .trivyignore  fixable HIGH/CRITICAL only, minus
#                                           this list → blocks the merge.
#
# The effect is that the gate blocks *regressions*. A newly disclosed CVE, or
# one a PR introduces via a dependency or base-image change, goes red on that
# PR. Debt that arrived with a third-party base image Atlas does not build stays
# visible in code scanning without pinning every unrelated PR red.
#
# ── Why there is a baseline at all ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every entry below was present in an upstream base image on the day the gate
# landed (#4822), and none of them was fixable by bumping a pin: the pinned
# digest for oven/bun:1.3.13 IS the current digest for that tag, so upstream has
# simply not rebuilt with the patched packages. "Fixable" in Trivy's sense means
# the distro has published a fixed package version — not that a fixed base image
# exists for us to move to.
#
# Clearing them needs a change to shipped artifacts (an apt upgrade in the
# runner stages, a rebase, or waiting for upstream rebuilds). That is deliberate
# product surface, and it was out of scope for the PR that added the gate, which
# landed during the 2026-07-27 ship freeze.
#
# ── How to clear it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Re-run the scan after any base-image change and delete every line that no
# longer appears. The expiry dates are the forcing function: once passed, Trivy
# stops honouring the entry and the gate goes red until it is either fixed or
# consciously re-dated. Do not bulk-extend the dates — re-scan first.
#
# Regenerate the current findings with:
#   bash scripts/list-runtime-base-images.sh | while read -r i; do \
#     trivy image --scanners vuln --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed \
#       --ignorefile /dev/null --format json "$i"; done
#
# Format: <ID> exp:<YYYY-MM-DD>. Comments are per-CVE below (severity, package).
# Entries are global rather than per-image on purpose — the two runtime bases
# share all 7 of the openssl-family CVEs, so per-image files would duplicate
# them and drift apart.
#
# Baseline captured 2026-07-27. Expiry 2026-09-01 (after the v0.2.0 release on
# 2026-08-03, with slack to schedule the base-image work).

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
